arliament  managed to push through  an unprecedented regulation called the  gdpr which is general data protection  regulation and last july we took a  historic vote on the digital services  act and the digital markets act with the  aim of making sure that consumers are  protected privacy is a foremost concern  but also the companies digital companies  technology companies on which we rely  but which we need to ensure continue to  be a force for good and not a force that  is abused of by autocracies by other  malign players in our world also  something that we are going to look  forward to the ai act the artificial  intelligence act you know ruta other  countries around the world look to the  european parliament and the european  union and look at our legislation in  order to try to push forward this  balance that we have found in this house  also in those countries i take that as a  big compliment but also one with big  responsibility because fundamental  rights what what was just asked in terms  of human rights when we are talking  about data how data is used but also how  it can also be a force for good and  research in technology in all areas uh i  was also i mentioned what you call i  visit for example medical institutions  how much they need to rely uh on on data  and privacy but also the technology and  the software needed and the investment  in in our researches in our universities  for us to have that competitive edge  over other  countries that have been raised with a  much different value set to uh to ours  that is what our legislation seeks to  strengthen we need to continue to do  more but now also the ball is in the  court of the governments that the votes  we take here are actually transformed  into rules that apply on the ground it's  not always easy and sometimes we take it  for granted but that's what the european  parliament is for  and uh well then it's time to talk about  the future  of the eu which is also  an important issue many people have  questions about it  and  let's have a look to another video  message from dave koch from austria  dear roberta my name is dave koch and  i'm from austria and i'm really curious  how you and the european parliament make  the eu stronger so that we're less  dependent on events that happen outside  of our borders like the gaza power  crisis  how can we make the eu stronger so i was  trying to release them the text is far  away um well  that's  something that you know you see behind  you one of the most amazing images that  was the moment when young people came  from all over europe and told us  political leaders how we should make the  eu better stronger internally when faced  with global challenges sometimes within  our borders sometimes also outside our  borders which is what we are facing now  i would answer uh mr mr koko just ask  the question that we should have acted  much quicker on the energy union that we  should be able to buy gas  or electricity and renewables from each  other rather than from frankly our  enemies i think now is the time for us  to do more also on the environmental  front but that picture also shows to me  a discussion that took place that we  need to discuss  and we can't say use the word future but  also we need to discuss the gaps  we have seen over the past two years  when we needed to act together on health  we did it even though it's not strictly  speaking a competence of the european  union when we needed to act together in  order to make sure that we grant as  quickly as possible macro financial  assistance to ukraine we did it when we  needed to be faster on adopting digital  legislation we did it faster than ever  before  so there is the political will today and  i would like to harness that political  will and that is why the parliament is  pushing for a convention in order to  fill those gaps are we not in time to  discuss is it not now the best time to  have an energy union is it not now the  best time to have a health union i think  that is what our citizens want from us  and that is what we need to deliver  and then the very last question again  about the future that comes from inigo  let's have a look to the screen  so an ego on facebook was saying that  every day more people are realizing all  the aspects of the eu but i also found  that the vast majority of people do not  know anything about the eu in your own  personal experience what is the best way  to proselytize the wonders and some  mistakes of the view  thank you and have a nice day he's  wishing to you so basically how can we  communicate better in the future about  the eu  i think we need to be honest  and i think we need to be open  we can sometimes we  we allow criticism to be leveled at the  european union when it is not the  european union's fault but sometimes we  don't say where we could have done  better  where we failed  and we don't say mostly where we have  succeeded so it's all boils down first  of all to openness transparency fairness  being  the home  not only if you live in a city in a big  metropolis but in any town and village  across the european union you need to  feel part of the european union and you  need to know that everything that is  happening  how you can pay your bills  how you are facing medical shortages how  you are going to make sure you have  equal access to health and education  that is thanks to the european union we  don't say that enough we don't say that  this is what the european union is doing  i come from a country where  people know who their members of the  european parliament are i would like  everybody to know who their members of  the european parliament are i would like  members from all countries and now  candidates as they gear up for the  election in 2024 to knock on doors we  don't only communicate virtually let's  after two years of being only online we  also need to meet in this kind of  setting we need to go to all doors to  all parts of europe and say we are here  talk to 